// Humpy: Jack Horner's deer-hair dry, also the Goofus Bug: one bunch of hair makes the hump over a thread belly and the wings.

id humpy
name "Humpy"
aliases "Yellow Humpy" "Goofus Bug" "Horner Deer Hair"
type dry
imitates attractor mayfly caddis
naturals mayfly-dun caddis-adult
difficulty advanced
species trout
season june july august september
origin originator="Jack Horner" region="California, USA"
description "A moose hair tail, a yellow thread belly under a hump of elk hair whose tips stand up as divided wings, and a brown and grizzly hackle."
history """
    Jack Horner of San Francisco tied it for California's swift rivers in the 1940s, early or
    late by different accounts, from the Tom Thumb's two clumps of deer hair over a thread
    body. Pat Barnes of West Yellowstone found two of them in a trout's jaw on Cliff Lake in
    1947, by Rusty Dunn's account (1943 in another), and his wife Sig copied them; as the Goofus
    Bug it became a Yellowstone staple, and around Jackson Hole it got the name Humpy. Its trick
    is the single bunch of hair: tied in pointing back, bound down over the thread belly,
    folded forward as the hump, its tips stood up as the wings. Flagler's shortcut uses two
    bunches, and Craven makes the hump from the butts of the wing.
    """
fishing """
    A big, buoyant attractor for fast, broken water on western rivers, where the hollow hair
    hump keeps it up through riffles and pocket water. Fish it dead drift; the yellow belly
    suggests a stonefly or a hatching caddis as much as a mayfly.
    """
sources {
    "Fly Fisherman: Fly Tier's Bench, the Humpy (Kathy Buchner, 1999)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/fly-tiers-bench-humpy/463293"
    "Orvis Learning Center: Yellow Humpy (Matt Grobert)" url="https://howtoflyfish.orvis.com/fly-tying-videos/dry-flies/704-yellow_humpy"
    "Fly Fisherman: Fly Tying the Humpy (Charlie Craven)" url="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/fly-tying-humpy/151877"
    "Fly Anglers Online: The Humpy, Fly of the Week #120 (Skip Morris)" url="https://www.flyanglersonline.com/t/the-humpy-fly-of-the-week-120/3770"
    "SWTU: Horner Deer Hair, Goofus Bug, Humpy (Rusty Dunn)" url="https://www.swtu.org/2018/01/04/horner-deer-hair-goofus-bug-humpy/"
    "Wikipedia: Humpy fly" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humpy_fly"
    "Fly Fishing the Sierra: Humpy" url="https://flyfishingthesierra.com/humpy/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://i0.wp.com/www.swtu.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/Humpy3-975.jpg?resize=975%2C709&ssl=1" page="https://www.swtu.org/2018/01/04/horner-deer-hair-goofus-bug-humpy/" author="Rusty Dunn" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise; the yellow belly and the hump clear"
    "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/Humpy_fly.jpg" page="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humpy_fly.jpg" author="Mike Cline" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" note="Side view in the vise"
    "https://content.osgnetworks.tv/flyfisherman/content/photos/TyingHumpy-HERO-1200x800.jpg" page="https://www.flyfisherman.com/editorial/fly-tying-humpy/151877" author="Charlie Craven" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise"
}

sits hackle
hook dry-standard size=12
// The thread is the belly that shows under the hump.
thread yellow size="6/0"

materials {
    tail moose-mane label="Moose body hair"
    hump elk-hair label="Elk body hair"
    hackle-brown cock-hackle color=brown fibre=1.6
    hackle-grizzly cock-hackle color=grizzly fibre=1.6
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.5
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Sources: a shank beyond the bend; the photos show about 1.8 gapes.
    tie-in tail length=1.8 spread=20 note="A small stacked clump on top."
    trim tail note="Trim the butts just past the middle of the shank and bind them: the base of the hump."
    tie-in hump length=3.6 angle=3 spread=40 note="Measured from the tail tips to the eye; butts at mid-shank, tips back, bound down to the tail."
    wrap-thread to=0.55 note="Back and forth over the hair: the yellow thread is the belly."
    // Sources: the wings about a shank tall; the hump about half a gape high in the photos.
    pull-over hump to=0.55 bubble=0.3 wing=2.0 note="Fold the hair forward over the top, tight and even, and bind it with a short band; stand the tips up and divide them."
    tie-in hackle-brown
    tie-in hackle-grizzly
    wrap-thread to=0.92
    wind hackle-brown to=0.9 turns=5 note="Two or three turns behind the wings, the rest in front."
    tie-off hackle-brown
    wind hackle-grizzly to=0.9 turns=5 note="Through the brown, the same way."
    tie-off hackle-grizzly
    whip-finish
}
